## PASSING checks (evidence)

1. **axe-core, zero NEW violations.** `axe.run(document, {runOnly:{type:'tag',
   values:['wcag2a','wcag2aa','wcag21a','wcag21aa','wcag22aa']}})` at all 4
   tag combos (375/1440 × light/dark) returned exactly one violation each
   time: `link-name` (serious) on `tr[data-category=""] > td:nth-child(1) >
   .underline` — an empty-text link inside the Spending-trends comparison
   table for a row with an empty category. Verified PRE-EXISTING (not
   introduced/replicated by IP1): `curl` of master's own render of the same
   fixture on :8100 contains the byte-identical row at the same position:
   `<tr data-category="" data-current="30" data-previous="0"
   data-change="30"><td class="p-3 text-sm"><a class="text-accent underline"
   href="/explorer?category=&amp;start=2025-07-01&amp;end=2025-12-31&amp;
   type=Outflow"></a></td>...`. This table is not in IP1's manifest. Reported
   below as an OBSERVATION only, per the pre-existing-markup rule.

2. **Heading order / landmarks** (ACCESSIBILITY.md §1). DOM query at 1440,
   both themes: exactly one `<h1>` ("Insights"), then `h2 > h3` throughout
   with no skipped level: `What changed(h2) > Largest contributors(h3)`,
   `Review these transactions(h2)`, `Detected recurring spending(h2) >
   Detected subscriptions/Recurring bills/Other recurring spending(h3 x3)`,
   `Supporting charts and tables(h2) > Spending trends/Income
   sources/Anomalies/Price Creep/Spending pace(h3 x5)`. Landmarks:
   `main=1, nav=2 (site nav + "On this page"), header=1, footer=1`.

3. **"On this page" nav landmark** (brief item). `nav[aria-label="On this
   page"]` found, 4 anchors (`#insights-change`, `#insights-findings`,
   `#insights-recurring`, `#insights-supporting`), each target either is a
   heading or contains one. Clicked the real `#insights-recurring` link at
   both viewports/themes and confirmed
   `getElementById('insights-recurring').getBoundingClientRect().top` lands
   between 0 and the viewport height (not hidden under overlapping chrome —
   confirmed separately the header is `position: static`, not
   fixed/sticky, so `scroll-mt-20` has no obstruction to correct for
   in this app in the first place).

4. **Meaning not by color alone** (ACCESSIBILITY.md §8). `document.body.
   innerText` contains the literal word "increase" (Change tile + Shopping/
   Auto & Transport/Charity contributor deltas) and the literal strings
   "High severity" / "Medium severity" on the anomaly badges, in both
   themes — confirmed present at 1440 both themes.

5. **`aria-hidden` decorative bars carry no focusable content.** All 3
   contributor-bar tracks (`div.h-1.5.w-full[aria-hidden="true"]`) contain
   zero `a/button/input/select/textarea/[tabindex]` descendants, both
   themes.

6. **Severity-badge text contrast ≥ 4.5:1** (ACCESSIBILITY.md §7), computed
   via `getComputedStyle` + WCAG relative-luminance formula, light theme:
   "Medium severity" `rgb(146,64,14)` on `rgb(255,251,235)` = 6.84:1;
   "High severity" `rgb(190,18,60)` on `rgb(255,241,242)` = 5.72:1. (Dark
   theme also computed and passed — see raw JSON below.)

7. **Bar-fill contrast ≥ 3:1** vs. card background: `rgb(190,18,60)` (the
   negative fill color) vs. white card = 6.29:1 in light theme; dark theme
   also passed (raw JSON below).

8. **Focus visible on new links** (ACCESSIBILITY.md §9). First pass with
   `.focus()` immediately after a mouse `.click()` in the SAME browser
   context spuriously reported no `:focus-visible` outline — traced this to
   Chromium's input-modality heuristic (a prior real mouse click suppresses
   `:focus-visible` on a subsequent programmatic `.focus()` in the same
   context; verified with a minimal throwaway repro,
   `$SCRATCH/probes/debug_focus.cjs`, that showed `outline: rgb(79,70,229)
   solid 2px` / real `box-shadow` on a FRESH page for the identical
   selector). Re-ran the real probe with the focus checks moved before the
   click-based anchor test (so `.focus()` is the first interaction in the
   context) and both the "On this page" nav link and the recurring-strip
   tile link then show a real, non-`none` `outline-style` / `box-shadow` on
   `:focus-visible`, in both themes.

9. **Target size ≥ 24 px** (WCAG 2.5.8 / brief item) on the new nav links
   and strip tiles, both viewports/themes: nav links measured `h≈29.75px`
   (all 4), strip tiles `h≈108–112px` — all well over 24px. No axe
   `target-size` violation either.

10. **Dashboard `/` unchanged.** `diff` of `curl http://127.0.0.1:8099/`
    (branch) vs `curl http://127.0.0.1:8100/` (master, same testdata/)
    returns byte-identical output (`diff` exit 0) — stronger evidence than a
    screenshot compare since it proves not just visual parity but zero
    markup drift.

11. **`Compact` did not leak into the `/insights/*` HTMX partials.**
    `curl -H "HX-Request: true"` on `/insights/recurring`,
    `/insights/trends`, `/insights/income` (same fixture) on branch vs.
    master: all three `diff` byte-identical (exit 0) — confirms §3.5's "the
    Dashboard, `/insights/*` partials ... must be byte-identical" invariant
    holds from the a11y-relevant angle (no accidental Compact/no-card-chrome
    bleed into a surface that still needs its own landmark/card semantics).

## FAILING check — ACCESSIBILITY.md §7 (non-text contrast ≥ 3:1)

The brief explicitly requires checking "tile borders ≥ 3:1 against the card
background." Five of the new neutral (non-directional) tiles use
`border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700` and fail this badly in BOTH themes:

```
1440-light: tile border[Selected period] contrast 1.26:1 < 3:1 (border=rgb(231,229,228) bg=rgb(255,255,255))
1440-light: tile border[Prior period]    contrast 1.26:1 < 3:1 (border=rgb(231,229,228) bg=rgb(255,255,255))
1440-light: tile border[Detected subscriptions] contrast 1.15:1 < 3:1 (border=rgb(231,229,228) bg=rgb(245,245,244))
1440-light: tile border[Recurring bills]        contrast 1.15:1 < 3:1 (border=rgb(231,229,228) bg=rgb(245,245,244))
1440-light: tile border[Other recurring spending] contrast 1.15:1 < 3:1 (border=rgb(231,229,228) bg=rgb(245,245,244))
1440-dark:  tile border[Selected period] contrast 1.48:1 < 3:1 (border=rgb(68,64,60) bg=rgb(41,37,36))
1440-dark:  tile border[Prior period]    contrast 1.48:1 < 3:1 (border=rgb(68,64,60) bg=rgb(41,37,36))
1440-dark:  tile border[Detected subscriptions] contrast 1.70:1 < 3:1 (border=rgb(68,64,60) bg=rgb(28,25,23))
1440-dark:  tile border[Recurring bills]        contrast 1.70:1 < 3:1 (border=rgb(68,64,60) bg=rgb(28,25,23))
1440-dark:  tile border[Other recurring spending] contrast 1.70:1 < 3:1 (border=rgb(68,64,60) bg=rgb(28,25,23))
```

(The colored variant of the Change tile — `border-negative`/`border-positive`
— is NOT affected; it passed. Only the 5 neutral-border tiles fail.)

Confirmed visually with real Chromium screenshots
(`$SCRATCH/shots/insights-1440-{light,dark}.png`): the "Selected period" /
"Prior period" tile boundaries and the recurring-strip tile boundaries read
as essentially borderless in both themes — there is no perceivable boundary
line separating a tile from its card background, which is exactly what the
1.15–1.7:1 measurement predicts. A low-vision user relying on the boundary
to tell where one tile ends and the next begins does not get one.

Attribution (git diff master, per the lead's classification rule):
```
$ rtk proxy git diff master -- web/templates/pages/insights.html | grep -n "border-gray-200\|rounded-xl"
23:+            <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 p-4">
27:+            <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 p-4">
31:+            <div class="rounded-xl border p-4 {{if eq .Change.Direction "up"}}border-negative bg-negative-soft{{else if eq .Change.Direction "down"}}border-positive bg-positive-soft{{else}}border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700{{end}}">
61:+            <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 p-4">
89:+            <a href="#recurring-{{.ID}}" class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 p-4 block focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-accent">
```
All 5 are `+` lines — genuinely new markup introduced by this task, not
pre-existing content this task merely touched. (Note for provenance only,
does not change the FAIL: the SAME low-contrast `border-gray-200
dark:border-gray-700` neutral-tile pattern already exists, uncontested, on
the Dashboard's `components/kpis.html` KPI tiles — so this is a systemic,
site-wide pattern the worker replicated rather than inventing from scratch.
Per the lead's own rule ("FAIL only what this task's change introduced or
replicated into new content") replication into IP1's new tiles is still a
FAIL, not an observation — but the Dashboard's own pre-existing instance is
out of IP1's scope and is called out below as a backlog item, not folded
into this FAIL.)

